<chapter
 id="lecture05">
#title("Functional List-Processing (1)")

#para('\

Lists are by far the most commonly used data structure in functional
programming: The functional programming language LISP is even named
after #emphasis("LISt Processor"). For someone with a background in
imperative programming, it is important to note that a functional list
is essentially an immutable linked-list: Such a list can never be
changed after its creation. In particular, functional list-processing
cannot modify any lists being processed. \

')

#para('\

There is no garbage collector (GC) in this dialect of ATS2, and
the lists of the GC era (the type #stacode("list0")) do not exist any more:
a list node lives on heap, and a value that lives on heap has to be
#emphasis("linear") (that is, there is exactly one owner who is responsible for
freeing it). The lists of this book are the linear lists of the prelude,
that is, the lists of the type #stacode("list_vt"). The functions that are
presented in this chapter keep the spirit of functional list-processing:
a list that is only read is
#emphasis("borrowed") (the symbol #keycode("!") in front of its type, which
means that the caller keeps the list and still owns it after the call) and
the result of a function is a newly constructed list, which belongs to
the caller, who is to free it in the end.

')

#para('\

In ATS, there is a datatype #stacode("list_vt") declared as follows
(the actual name of the type constructor is a long one, and
#stacode("list_vt") is a shorthand of it):

#sats2xhtml('\
//
datavtype
list_vt0ype_int_vtype
  (a:vt@ype+, int) =
  | list_vt_nil(a, 0) of ()
  | {n:int | n >= 0}
    list_vt_cons(a, n+1) of (a, list_vt0ype_int_vtype(a, n))
// end of [list_vt0ype_int_vtype]
//
')

Note that #stacode("vt@ype") is a sort for static terms representing
types of dynamic values of unspecified size that may be linear. Given a type
#stacode("T") and a natural number #stacode("n"), #stacode("list_vt(T, n)") is
for lists of length #stacode("n") consisting of elements of the type
#stacode("T"). The type #stacode("List0_vt(T)") of the prelude is for lists of
an unspecified length and it is the type that is used when the length does not
matter.

')

#para('\

Every datavtype is associated with a set of data-constructors, which
are called for constructing (boxed) values of the datatype. The
declaration of #stacode("list_vt") indicates that #dyncode("list_vt_nil")
and #dyncode("list_vt_cons") are the two constructors assocated with
#stacode("list_vt"); #dyncode("list_vt_nil") is nullary;
#dyncode("list_vt_cons") is binary, which takes a given element and a
given list to form a new list such that the given element and list are
the head and tail of the newly formed list, respectively. For instance,
the following code binds #dyncode("xs3") to a list of type
#stacode("list_vt(int, 3)") that contains three elements 3, 2, and 1:

#dats2xhtml('\
val xs0 = list_vt_nil{int}() // xs = ()
val xs1 = list_vt_cons{int}(1, xs0) // xs = (1)
val xs2 = list_vt_cons{int}(2, xs1) // xs = (2, 1)
val xs3 = list_vt_cons{int}(3, xs2) // xs = (3, 2, 1)
')

Constructing a list with #dyncode("list_vt_cons") consumes its tail:
the variables #dyncode("xs0"), #dyncode("xs1") and #dyncode("xs2") are
not available any more once they are used. The static argument
#dyncode("{int}") tells the typechecker that the element type of the list
is #stacode("int"). Another notation for constructing a list is used in the
following sample code:

#dats2xhtml('\
val xs123 = $list_vt{int}(1, 2, 3) // xs123 = (1, 2, 3)
val ys123 = $list_vt{string}("1", "2", "3") // ys123 = ("1", "2", "3")
')

Note that #dyncode("$list_vt{T}(...)") is for constructing a list-value
in which each element is of type T. In the absence of GC, a list has to be freed
by its owner, and the function #dyncode("list_vt_free") of the prelude does
so for a list of non-linear elements. For instance, we can print out the list
#dyncode("xs3") (printing borrows the list) and free it as follows:

#dats2xhtml('\
val () = println! ("xs3 = ", xs3)
val () = println! ("length(xs3) = ", list_length<int>(xs3))
//
val () = list_vt_free<int>(xs3)
')

If the last line is dropped, the compiler complains that the linear variable
#dyncode("xs3") is not consumed. And if the list is used after the last line,
the compiler complains that it is not available any more.

')

#para('\

The function for computing the length of a given list
can be implemented as follows (the functions of the chapter, which
form a part of the library of the book, are collected in the file
#filename("listfun.hats")):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
list_length
  {n:nat}(xs: !list_vt(a, n)): int(n)
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
list_length(xs) =
(
case+ xs of
| list_vt_nil() => 0
| list_vt_cons(x0, xs1) => 1 + list_length<a>(xs1)
) (* end of [list_length] *)
//
')

Strictly speacking, #dyncode("list_length") is a function template
(rather than a function). In the case-expression, there are two
pattern matching clauses; each clause consists of a guard, which is
pattern, and a body, which is an expression. When the value
#dyncode("xs") matches the guard of a clause, the body of the clause
is chosen for subsequent evaluation. If #dyncode("xs") is empty,
then it is constructed with #dyncode("list_vt_nil") and thus matches the
pattern #dyncode("list_vt_nil()").  If #dyncode("xs") is non-empty,
then then it is constructed with #dyncode("list_vt_cons") and thus
matches the pattern #dyncode("list_vt_cons(x0, xs1)"), resulting in the
names #dyncode("x0") and #dyncode("xs1") bound to the head and tail of
#dyncode("xs"), respectively. As #dyncode("xs") is borrowed, the pattern
matching only reads it, and the length is returned as an integer
of the singleton type #stacode("int(n)") (this is how the typechecker knows
that the result of a call on a list of length n is n).

')

#para('\

Though the given implementation of #dyncode("list_length") is not
tail-recursive, it should be clear that a tail-recursive
implementation can be readily done. Note that the function
#dyncode("list_length") is of O(n)-time for n being the length of its
argument. Often I see someone writing the code
#dyncode("list_length(xs) > 0") for testing whether a given list
#dyncode("xs") is empty. This practice is terribly inefficient as
checking whether a list is empty can be easily done in O(1)-time.

')

#para('\

The function for concatenating two given lists can be implemented
as follows:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
list_append
  {m,n:nat}
( xs: !list_vt(a, m)
, ys: !list_vt(a, n)): list_vt(a, m+n)
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
list_append(xs, ys) =
(
case+ xs of
| list_vt_nil() => list_vt_copy<a>(ys)
| list_vt_cons(x, xs) => list_vt_cons(x, list_append<a>(xs, ys))
) (* end of [list_append] *)
//
')

Given #dyncode("xs") and #dyncode("ys"), #dyncode("list_append")
returns a list that represents the concatenation of #dyncode("xs") and
#dyncode("ys"). Clearly, #dyncode("list_append") is O(n)-time for n
being the length of #dyncode("xs") plus the length of #dyncode("ys")
(since a list cannot be shared in the absence of GC, the elements of
#dyncode("ys") are copied by #dyncode("list_vt_copy")). The implementation of
#dyncode("list_append") is functional in the sense that it does not
alter either #dyncode("xs") or #dyncode("ys") for the construction of
the concatenation: they are both borrowed. For clarification, I point out that there is a
function of the name #dyncode("list_vt_append") in the prelude that consumes
two given linear lists to construct their concatenation in O(n)-time without
copying. When a call to #dyncode("list_vt_append") returns, the two linear lists
passed to the call are no longer available for subsequent use.

')

#para('\

The function for constructing the reverse of a given list
can be implemented as follows:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
list_revappend
  {m,n:nat}
( xs: !list_vt(a, m)
, ys: list_vt(a, n)): list_vt(a, m+n)
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
list_reverse
  {n:nat}(xs: !list_vt(a, n)): list_vt(a, n)
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
list_revappend(xs, ys) =
(
case+ xs of
| list_vt_nil() => ys
| list_vt_cons(x, xs) => list_revappend<a>(xs, list_vt_cons(x, ys))
) (* end of [list_revappend] *)
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
list_reverse(xs) = list_revappend<a>(xs, list_vt_nil())
//
')

Given a list #dyncode("xs"), #dyncode("list_reverse")
returns a newly contructed list that represents the reverse of
#dyncode("xs"). Given two lists #dyncode("xs") and
#dyncode("ys"), #dyncode("list_revappend") returns a newly
contructed list that represents the concatenation of the reverse of
#dyncode("xs") and #dyncode("ys"); the list #dyncode("ys") is
consumed (it becomes a part of the result) and the list #dyncode("xs")
is borrowed. Clearly, both #dyncode("list_reverse") and
#dyncode("list_revappend") are O(n)-time functions, where n is the length of
#dyncode("xs"), and they are tail-recursive.

')

#para('\

A commonly used list-combinator is the #dyncode("list_foldleft")
function implemented as follows:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{r:vt@ype}
{a:t@ype}
list_foldleft
( xs: !List0_vt(a)
, r0: r, fopr: &(r, a) -<clo1> r): r
//
implement
{r}{a}
list_foldleft
( xs
, r0, fopr) =
loop(xs, r0, fopr) where
{
fun
loop
(
xs: !List0_vt(a), r0: r, fopr: &(r, a) -<clo1> r
) : r =
(
case+ xs of
| list_vt_nil() => r0
| list_vt_cons(x, xs) => loop(xs, fopr(r0, x), fopr)
) (* end of [loop] *)
}
//
')

where the elements in the given list #dyncode("xs") are processed from
left to right. Clearly, #dyncode("list_foldleft") is tail-recursive. The sort
#stacode("vt@ype") of #stacode("r") means that the accumulator can be a
linear value (for instance, a list that is being built), which is passed to
#dyncode("fopr") and replaced by the value that is returned (and thus it is
never lost or duplicated). The function argument #dyncode("fopr") is a
flat closure that is passed by reference (see the chapter on higher-order
functions): it is created with #keycode("lam@") in the stack-frame of the caller
and nothing needs to be freed.

')

#para('\

As an example, the function for computing the length of
a given list can be implemented with a direct
call to #dyncode("list_foldleft"):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
list_length2(xs: !List0_vt(a)): int
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
list_length2(xs) = let
  var fopr = lam@(r: int, _: a): int => r + 1
in
  list_foldleft<int><a>(xs, 0, fopr)
end // end of [list_length2]
//
')

As another example, the reverse-append function that returns the link
of the reverse of a given list with another given list
can also be implemented with a direct call to #dyncode("list_foldleft"):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
list_revappend2
(xs: !List0_vt(a), ys: List0_vt(a)): List0_vt(a)
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
list_revappend2(xs, ys) = let
  var fopr = lam@(ys: List0_vt(a), x: a): List0_vt(a) => list_vt_cons(x, ys)
in
  list_foldleft<List0_vt(a)><a>(xs, ys, fopr)
end // end of [list_revappend2]
//
')

')

#para('\

In contrast to #dyncode("list_foldleft"), the following function
#dyncode("list_foldright") processes the elements in a given list
from right to left:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
{r:vt@ype}
list_foldright
( xs: !List0_vt(a)
, fopr: &(a, r) -<clo1> r, r0: r): r
//
implement
{a}{r}
list_foldright
( xs
, fopr, r0) =
auxlst(xs, fopr, r0) where
{
fun
auxlst
(
xs: !List0_vt(a), fopr: &(a, r) -<clo1> r, r0: r
) : r =
(
case+ xs of
| list_vt_nil() => r0
| list_vt_cons(x, xs) => fopr(x, auxlst(xs, fopr, r0))
) (* end of [auxlst] *)
}
//
')

Please note that #dyncode("list_foldright") is not tail-recursive.
As an example, the append function for concatenating two given
lists can be implmented with a direct call to
#dyncode("list_foldright"):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
list_append2
(xs: !List0_vt(a), ys: !List0_vt(a)): List0_vt(a)
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
list_append2(xs, ys) = let
  var fopr = lam@(x: a, ys: List0_vt(a)): List0_vt(a) => list_vt_cons(x, ys)
in
  list_foldright<a><List0_vt(a)>(xs, fopr, list_vt_copy<a>(ys))
end // end of [list_append2]
//
')

As another example, the list-concatenate function for concatenating
a list of lists is given as follows. A list of lists is linear (the elements
are lists), and it is consumed: every list in it is appended to the
following ones, and no copy is needed:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
list_concat(xss: List0_vt(List0_vt(a))): List0_vt(a)
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
list_concat(xss) =
(
case+ xss of
| ~list_vt_nil() => list_vt_nil()
| ~list_vt_cons(xs, xss) => list_vt_append<a>(xs, list_concat<a>(xss))
) (* end of [list_concat] *)
//
')

The symbol #keycode("~") in front of a pattern means that the matched
list node is freed (and the matched value is no longer available) while its
components, which are all owned by the matched value, are bound to the
variables of the pattern and are now owned by the program.

')

#para('\

In a case where a function (for example, #dyncode("list_length2"))
can be implemented with a call to either #dyncode("list_foldleft")
or #dyncode("list_foldright"), it is clear (unless there is a very
special reason) that the former should be chosen as it is
tail-recursive but the latter is not.

')

#para('\

Often I see a beginner of functional programming giving
the following implementation of #dyncode("list_reverse"):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
list_reverse2(xs: !List0_vt(a)): List0_vt(a)
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
list_reverse2(xs) =
(
case+ xs of
| list_vt_nil() => list_vt_nil()
| list_vt_cons(x, xs) =>
  list_vt_append<a>(list_reverse2<a>(xs), list_vt_cons(x, list_vt_nil()))
)
//
')

While this implementation is functionally correctly, it is of
O(n#sup("2"))-time complexity and thus can take a prohibitively long
time for an input that is not so short (e.g., one containing 10K
elements).

')

#para('\

As the last example of this chapter,
#dyncode("list_insertion_sort") is implemented
as follows that applies insertion sort to a given
list to return a sorted version of the list:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
list_insertion_sort
(
xs: !List0_vt(a), cmp: &(a, a) -<clo1> int
) : List0_vt(a)
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
list_insertion_sort
  (xs, cmp) = let
//
fun
insord
( x0: a
, xs: List0_vt(a)
, cmp: &(a, a) -<clo1> int): List0_vt(a) =
(
case+ xs of
| ~list_vt_nil() =>
  list_vt_sing(x0)
| ~list_vt_cons(x1, xs1) =>
  (
    if cmp(x0, x1) < 0
      then list_vt_cons(x0, list_vt_cons(x1, xs1))
      else list_vt_cons(x1, insord(x0, xs1, cmp))
    // end of [if]
  ) // end of [list_vt_cons]
) (* end of [insord] *)
//
fun
loop
( xs: !List0_vt(a)
, res: List0_vt(a)
, cmp: &(a, a) -<clo1> int): List0_vt(a) =
(
case+ xs of
| list_vt_nil() => res
| list_vt_cons(x, xs) => loop(xs, insord(x, res, cmp), cmp)
) (* end of [loop] *)
//
in
//
loop(xs, list_vt_nil(), cmp)
//
end // end of [list_insertion_sort]
//
')

Note that the argument #dyncode("cmp") is a comparion function that is
expected to return -1, 1, or 0 when its first argument is less than,
greater than, or equal to its second argument, respectively.
The loop is written directly instead of being a call to
#dyncode("list_foldleft"): the closure #dyncode("cmp") is passed by reference
and cannot be captured by another closure, which a call to
#dyncode("list_foldleft") would need.

')

#para('\

A call to #dyncode("list_insertion_sort") may look like:

#dats2xhtml('\
var cmp = lam@(x: int, y: int): int => compare(x, y)
val ys = list_insertion_sort<int>(xs, cmp)
val () = println! ("sorted = ", ys)
')

where #dyncode("xs") is a list of integers. Both #dyncode("xs") and
the result #dyncode("ys") are lists, and the program is to free both of them.

')

#para('\

Please find #mycodelink("LECTURE/05/CODE", "on-line") the entirety of
the code used in this chapter. It was compiled with #command("patscc")
(no flag is needed) and run with the address sanitizer, which reports
no leak. \

')#comment("para")

</chapter><!--id="lecture05"-->

